                <pb facs="0942" n="897" xml:id="pg897"/>

                <head>341. Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Brzezinski) to Secretary of State
                        Muskie<anchor n="1" xml:id="d341-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1980-12-18">December 18, 1980</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The President has read an intelligence report reflecting Soviet concern over
                    passage of the anti-Soviet Afghan resolution at the UN.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d341-f2"/> The Soviets are anxious lest
                    they lose non-aligned support over the Afghan issue. The intelligence community
                    has initiated planning to make Afghanistan a major issue at the February 9–12,
                    1981, Non-Aligned Nations Foreign Ministers’ Conference in New Delhi and
                    suggests that it is not too early to energize the entire U.S. foreign policy establishment toward the goal of defeating the
                    Soviets in New Delhi. The President noted, “I agree.”<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d341-f3"/> (S)</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Zbigniew
                                Brzezinski</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d341-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d341-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Bureau of Intelligence and Research, Intelligence
                    Liaison Files, TIN 980643000012, Box 6, Afghanistan 1980. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d341-f2" n="2" xml:id="d341-f2-content">
                <p>A likely reference to an article in the National Intelligence Daily that
                    summarized a report [<hi rendition="#italic">text not declassified</hi>]
                    December 2, which noted that the Soviets sought to “vitiate” the resolution
                    adopted on Afghanistan in the UN General
                    Assembly. (Central Intelligence Agency, Office of the Director of Central
                    Intelligence, Job 81B00401R: Subject Files of the Presidential Briefing
                    Coordinator for DCI (1977–81), Box 7,
                    Afghanistan Crisis—December 1980, NIDs) For UNGA Resolution 35/37, see <ref target="#d337-f3">footnote 3,
                        Document 337</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d341-f3" n="3" xml:id="d341-f3-content">
                <p>At the bottom of the memorandum, an unknown hand wrote: “CIA/DDO wanted to do something
                    through the CA infrastructure, but NEA (Coon)
                    was unenthusiastic, and nothing more was done.” The notation is dated February
                    9, 1981.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
